{
  "id": "ff9d1c3f-9fcb-471d-bfd6-5ecaa021412b",
  "productId": "openmrs",
  "title": "UserService.getUserByUuid Impact in REST Module",
  "updatedAt": 1793548528980,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "fZ7zAlYxaSwg26ef",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "If the behaviour of `UserService.getUserByUuid` in openmrs-core changed, which code in the REST web services module (openmrs-module-webservices.rest) uses it and could be affected?",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "`UserService.getUserByUuid(uuid)` is a lookup that turns a UUID (OpenMRS's globally unique string ID) into a `User` object. Inside openmrs-core it is only a thin read pass-through: the interface declares it [1], the implementation just delegates to the DAO in a read-only transaction [2], and the Hibernate DAO trims the string and looks the entity up by UUID, returning `null` when the argument is `null` [3]. So any behaviour change to this method (different null handling, different not-found result, permission/authentication checks, or a changed return type) propagates straight into the REST module.",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Confirmed callers in openmrs-module-webservices.rest",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "There are **five production call sites** (the rest are tests). Each was read:",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "| File | Where it calls | How it depends on the behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| `UserResource1_8.getByUniqueId(String)` | [4] | Wraps the result in `UserAndPassword1_8`; treats `null` as \"not found\" by returning `null` |\n| `UserConverter1_8.getByUniqueId(String)` | [5] | Returns the `User` as-is; `null` flows outward |\n| `ProviderSearchHandler1_9.search(RequestContext)` | [6] | Explicitly checks `user == null` → returns `EmptySearchResult`, else reads `user.getPerson()` |\n| `AlertRecipientResource2_0.setRecipientProperty(...)` | [7] | Only assigns when the looked-up user is non-null, otherwise silently leaves the old recipient |\n| `ChangePassword1_8.changeOthersPassword(...)` | [8] | `null` or missing user id triggers `NullPointerException`, mapped to HTTP 404 [9] |",
          "citations": [
            4,
            5,
            6,
            7,
            8,
            9
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## One hop outward: the public REST endpoints",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The framework calls `getByUniqueId` generically, so these endpoints reach the method indirectly:",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- **`GET /ws/rest/v1/user/{uuid}`** — `DelegatingCrudResource.retrieve` calls `getByUniqueId(uuid)` and throws `ObjectNotFoundException` when it is `null` [10]. Because `UserResource2_0` and `UserResource2_2` extend `UserResource1_8` and do not override it [11] [12], that path covers user reads on OpenMRS 1.8 through 9.x, not only 1.8. The same inherited methods are used for `DELETE` and `purge` [13].\n- **Any resource property of type `User` given as a UUID string** — `ConversionUtil` looks up a registered `Converter` and calls `converter.getByUniqueId(string)` [14], which is exactly `UserConverter1_8` [15]. This is the conversion path used when a request body or sub-resource contains a user UUID.\n- **`GET /ws/rest/v1/provider?user={userUuid}`** — the search handler above [16].\n- **Alert-recipient writes** — `AlertRecipientResource2_0` is a sub-resource of `AlertResource2_0` [17], reached through `DelegatingSubResource` [18].\n- **`POST /ws/rest/v1/password/{userUuid}`** — the change-others-password controller, which swallows the result into a 404 when the user is missing [8].",
          "citations": [
            10,
            11,
            12,
            13,
            14,
            15,
            16,
            17,
            18,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: Paths that reach UserService.getUserByUuid from the REST module\n---\nflowchart TD\n  restGetUser[\"GET /ws/rest/v1/user/{uuid}\"] --> retrieve[\"DelegatingCrudResource.retrieve\"]\n  restDeleteUser[\"DELETE /ws/rest/v1/user/{uuid}\"] --> delegCrud[\"DelegatingCrudResource\"]\n  restProps[\"Any User property given as UUID\"] --> convUtil[\"ConversionUtil\"]\n  restProvider[\"GET /ws/rest/v1/provider?user=\"] --> provHandler[\"ProviderSearchHandler1_9\"]\n  restAlert[\"Alert recipient writes\"] --> alertRes[\"AlertRecipientResource2_0\"]\n  restPwd[\"POST /ws/rest/v1/password/{uuid}\"] --> pwdCtrl[\"ChangePasswordController1_8\"]\n  retrieve --> userRes[\"UserResource1_8.getByUniqueId\"]\n  delegCrud --> userRes\n  convUtil --> userConv[\"UserConverter1_8.getByUniqueId\"]\n  userRes --> svc[\"UserService.getUserByUuid\"]\n  userConv --> svc\n  provHandler --> svc\n  alertRes --> svc\n  pwdCtrl --> svc\n  userResDerived[\"UserResource2_0 / 2_2 inherit\"] --> userRes\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Why the code is shaped this way",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The REST layer never queries the database itself; it goes through `Context.getUserService()` and relies on the service returning `null` for an unknown UUID. That means the module's behaviour is defined by the *contract*, not by the query — which is why each caller handles `null` differently: the user resource treats it as \"not found\", the provider search returns an empty page, the alert setter silently ignores it, and the password controller turns it into a 404. A change that made the method throw instead of returning `null` (or vice versa) would hit `ProviderSearchHandler1_9` and `AlertRecipientResource2_0` hardest, since both depend on the `null` signal.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## What I did not verify",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "I only searched `github.com/openmrs/openmrs-module-webservices.rest` for direct references, and I did not run the tests. I did not check other selected repositories (for example, `openmrs-module-fhir2`, which also consumes `UserService`) — the impact there could be similar but is out of scope for this question. `UserResource1_9Test` exists, but there is no `UserResource1_9` class in the module; the 1.9-era behaviour is handled by `UserResource1_8` itself and its 2.x subclasses. The matching `find_symbol_references` result also returned test-only usages (`UserController1_8Test`, `UserController2_0Test`, `PasswordResetController2_2Test`, `AlertController2_0Test`, and others), which would break at test time rather than at runtime if the contract changed.",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
